<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jahangir</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1569">1569</a>–<a href="/years/1627">1627</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Prince Salim</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jahangir was the fourth Mughal emperor, reigning from 1605 to 1627. The son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>, born Prince Salim, he largely kept his father's system of government, made peace with Mewar in 1615 and received the English envoy Sir Thomas Roe. A connoisseur of painting and nature, he wrote a candid memoir, while his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-jahan">Nur Jahan</a> held great influence at court.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jahangir.jpg" alt="Jahangir" width="320" height="428" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Abu al-Hasan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jahangircrop.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Prince Salim was born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatehpur-sikri">Fatehpur Sikri</a> on 31 August 1569, the third and only surviving son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> and Maryam uz-Zamani. His two elder brothers, the twins Hassan Mirza and Hussain Mirza, had died in infancy, and Akbar sought the blessing of the religious leader Salim Chishti, who lived at Fatehpur Sikri. Salim studied Chagatai, Persian and Hindustani as well as military tactics.</p><p>In 1594 he led an army against a revolt of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bundela">Bundela</a> at Orchha, and after their leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vir-singh-deo">Vir Singh Deo</a> surrendered, Orchha became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> state. In 1599, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> was campaigning in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>, Salim declared himself emperor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prayagraj">Allahabad</a> and issued coins in his own name. The rebellion ended without a decisive battle, and by 1604 peace was restored through the mediation of nobles such as Man Singh of Amber.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Jahangir succeeded to the throne on 3 November 1605. In April 1606 his son Khusrau rebelled, left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a> with 350 horsemen and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>, but Jahangir relieved the city and defeated him at Bhairowal. Khusrau was captured while crossing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chenab">Chenab</a>, and Jahangir had his young aristocratic supporters tortured and impaled as a warning. Khusrau was later blinded and imprisoned until 1619.</p><p>From his marriage to Mehr-un-Nissa, later Empress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-jahan">Nur Jahan</a>, Jahangir left the reins of government largely in her hands and appointed her relatives to high positions, which displeased courtiers and foreigners. He was famed for his Chain of Justice, by which subjects denied justice could appeal to the emperor, and he ordered the foundation of free hospitals in the cities of his empire. He recorded his reign and the natural world in his memoir, and commissioned painters such as Ustad Mansur to illustrate it. The editor Henry Beveridge, however, judged him a weak ruler who lost Qandahar to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>A lifelong user of opium and wine, Jahangir was frequently ill in the 1620s. He died in October 1627 near Bhimber, on the journey from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>. His entrails were buried at Baghsar Fort, and his body was taken to Lahore and buried at Shahdara Bagh, where his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a> commissioned his tomb. Shah Jahan succeeded him after a brief succession crisis. The tomb site was placed on the tentative list for UNESCO World Heritage status in 1993.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jahangir” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It set up a factory at Masulipatnam in 1611, gained rights at Surat from the Mughal Emperor <b>Jahangir</b> in 1612, built a factory at Madras in 1640 and leased Bombay island in 1668; after the Anglo-Mughal War Aurangzeb let it settle at Calcutta.</blockquote><a href="/empire/company-rule-in-india">Company rule in India · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The relationship with the Mughal Empire was defined by the treaty of 1615, when Amar Singh I accepted <b>Jahangir</b>’s overlordship through Prince Khurram; Mewar was spared the marriage alliances made by other Rajput houses, the Maharana was excused personal attendance at court and his heir served in his…</blockquote><a href="/empire/udaipur-state">Udaipur State · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The growing community attracted the suspicion of the Mughal authorities, and in 1606 Guru Arjan was executed under the emperor <b>Jahangir</b>, becoming the first Sikh martyr.</blockquote><a href="/religion/sikhism">Sikhism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>In law the emperor was the final judge: <b>Jahangir</b> hung a “chain of justice” in the Agra Fort that any wronged subject could shake.</blockquote><a href="/societies/mughal-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Mughal Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>Kishangarh was founded in 1611 by Kishan Singh, a son of Raja Udai Singh of Marwar, who received the territory near Ajmer from the Mughal emperor <b>Jahangir</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kishangarh-state">Kishangarh State</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 16th century they moved their seat into the hills at Dhameri, which was renamed Nurpur in the early 17th century after the emperor <b>Jahangir</b>, Nur-ud-din.</blockquote><a href="/state/nurpur-state">Nurpur State</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatehpur-sikri">Fatehpur Sikri</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in Rajauri, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=27.09,77.66&amp;year=1606" aria-label="Fatehpur Sikri on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Jahangir"><image href="/maps/globes/g090n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4068 5198h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4068 5198h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=27.09,77.66&amp;year=1606">What was here in 1606? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-family"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Family</h2><ul class="enc-seealso enc-fam"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/akbar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/akbar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Akbar</span><span class="tp">Father &#183; r. 1556–1605</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/nur-jahan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/nur-jahan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Nur Jahan</span><span class="tp">Married &#183; 1577–1645</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/shah-jahan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Shah Jahan</span><span class="tp">Child &#183; 1592–1666</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="ft-note">Parents, spouses and children as Wikidata records them, among the people of the encyclopedia: not the whole family.</p></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;After my accession, the first order that I gave was for the fastening up of the Chain of Justice…&#8221;</p><footer>Tuzuk-i-Jahangiri (his memoirs), on his accession, 1605 &#183; tr. Alexander Rogers, ed. Henry Beveridge, 1909 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/53674/pg53674-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…although I myself drink wine, and from the age of 18 years up till now, when I am 38, have persisted in it.&#8221;</p><footer>Tuzuk-i-Jahangiri (his memoirs), his edict against making wine &#183; tr. Alexander Rogers, ed. Henry Beveridge, 1909 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/53674/pg53674-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The good qualities of my revered father are beyond the limit of approval and the bounds of praise.&#8221;</p><footer>Tuzuk-i-Jahangiri (his memoirs), on his father &#183; tr. Alexander Rogers, ed. Henry Beveridge, 1909 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/53674/pg53674-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
